Found while implementing karr #40; documented there rather than changed, so
that #40 stayed a data-loss fix.

Since #40 every method that writes a file does it with a temporary file and a
rename. rename() checks write permission on the DIRECTORY, not on the file, so
a read-only file in a writable directory is replaced without complaint.

Measured, sops 3.13.3:

  chmod 0444 ro.yaml
  sops -e -i ro.yaml
  -> "Could not open in-place file for writing: open .../ro.yaml: permission
      denied", the file is untouched

  perl -e "rename(tmp, ro.yaml)"   -> succeeds, ro.yaml replaced

So where sops refuses, we proceed. The house rule is "where the reference
implementation refuses, refuse", which argues for a check; the counter-argument
is that this is how every atomic writer behaves and that a caller who chmodded
a file 0444 was defending against an accident, not mounting a real guard.

Before #40 the old open ">" path DID refuse it, by accident -- so this is a
behaviour change that shipped inside #40 undeclared except in the POD. Either
add the check (stat the target, croak naming the file, matching sops wording)
or leave it and make sure the Changes entry says so, not only the POD.

Note it applies to encrypt_in_place and edit as well, which have behaved this
way since karr #4 and never mentioned it.
The Changes entry written for #40 already declares this ('replacing a file needs write permission on its DIRECTORY rather than on the file (a read-only file in a writable directory is replaced, where sops -e -i refuses)'), so the open question is only whether to add the check, not whether it is announced.
